1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:02,430
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:05,090
This is the eleventh episode of Earthlings
101.

3
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:08,639
Today we will learn about something that is
so central to earthlings' life that they are

4
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:44,289
barely ever aware of it: Rituals.

5
00:00:44,289 --> 00:00:48,809
When you observe groups of earthlings, you
sometimes see behaviours that obviously serve

6
00:00:48,809 --> 00:00:52,430
the purpose of feeding, reproducing, and not
dying.

7
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:56,670
But often you have no idea what in the universe
is going on: They do things which have absolutely

8
00:00:56,670 --> 00:01:00,769
no practical sense, and make sounds that carry
no useful information.

9
00:01:00,769 --> 00:01:04,489
When this happens, odds are that you are actually
observing a ritual.

10
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:10,910
By the way, there are as many definitions
of "rituals" as there are anthropologists.

11
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:16,390
What I describe here is what earthling anthropologists
call, "interaction rituals".

12
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:18,540
Rituals are a bit like common tasks.

13
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,230
When a group of earthlings has a common task
to accomplish, they concentrate all together

14
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:27,240
onto this task, share their mood and coordinate
their actions, until the task is done.

15
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:30,650
As a side effect, they earn satisfaction and
an increased group spirit.

16
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:36,720
Now, a ritual is actually the same thing as
a common task - only without the common task.

17
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:40,650
To understand rituals, we have first to understand
emotions.

18
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:41,890
What are emotions?

19
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:46,770
An emotion is a positive or negative mental
state linked to a thing, creature, action,

20
00:01:46,770 --> 00:01:50,260
event or idea - like a virtual happyfield
or unhappyfield.

21
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:54,630
The four most important emotions are joy,
fear, sadness and anger.

22
00:01:54,630 --> 00:01:57,500
I'll talk more about emotions in another episode.

23
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:01,750
For now, the important thing to know is that
earthlings have an emotion-driven intelligence:

24
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:05,180
They try to move towards happyfields and away
from unhappyfields.

25
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:09,440
But earthlings can't share their emotions
and intentions telepathically.

26
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:14,450
So, the question is: If every earthling is
driven by his own emotions, how do earthlings

27
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:16,860
manage to accomplish common tasks?

28
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:21,430
How do they coordinate actions, and more importantly:
How do they coordinate the emotions that drive

29
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:22,920
those actions?

30
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:28,540
The answer is what might be the single most
important social mechanism on earth: Rituals.

31
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:33,040
In general earthlings are not aware that they
perform rituals on a daily basis.

32
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:36,450
Most of them will associate the word "ritual"
with a group of hooded men, performing some

33
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:38,920
secret ceremony in a poorly lit room.

34
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:43,340
But actually, rituals are omnipresent in everyday's
life.

35
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:44,650
Earthlings greeting each other?

36
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:45,670
A ritual.

37
00:02:45,670 --> 00:02:48,500
A social gathering, a group of musicians?

38
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:49,820
Rituals.

39
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,310
Earthlings eating together or playing a game?

40
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:53,310
Also rituals.

41
00:02:53,310 --> 00:02:54,850
A political gathering?

42
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:57,840
A sports event ? All rituals.

43
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,620
Some rituals are religious, but most are quite
profane.

44
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:02,730
A chat between friends?

45
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:05,020
Love-birds making sweet love?

46
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:06,959
Again, rituals.

47
00:03:06,959 --> 00:03:09,500
Earthlings attending a funeral ? A ritual.

48
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,610
But even social activities that serve an actual
purpose are kind of rituals:

49
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:18,120
Negociations, transactions, and any kind of
cooperative work, from daily working routine

50
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:20,950
to complex and risky work projects.

51
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:25,560
Actually, it's hard to find a social activity
that is not a ritual!

52
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:28,140
But let's go back to the hooded men in their
crypt.

53
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:32,150
This scene is good for explaining what happens
in a ritual - not because it's a frequent

54
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:35,830
event, but because earthlings think it is
exemplary for rituals.

55
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:38,130
So, what constitutes a ritual?

56
00:03:38,130 --> 00:03:42,880
To perform a ritual, we need earthlings gathering
together, and separating themselves from the

57
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:44,720
exterior world.

58
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:49,550
The ritual usually follows predefined formalities
- more or less strict rules what outfit to

59
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:51,980
wear, what objects to use etc.

60
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:55,990
The earthlings concentrate on the same thing
and perform some predefined actions.

61
00:03:55,990 --> 00:04:00,879
The thing they concentrate upon becomes the
center of the ritual, a "sacret object".

62
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:06,410
It can be an actual object, but also a person,
an idea, or whatever else can be a "thing".

63
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:11,180
The ritual actions are usually coordinated,
often rhythmic activities, like processions,

64
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:14,060
chants, formulae or body movements.

65
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:18,250
What matters is actually not the actions per
se, but the coordination, the common focus

66
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:20,060
onto the sacret object.

67
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:24,999
This ritual activity helps establishing a
shared emotion: Devotion, joy, pain, anger

68
00:04:24,999 --> 00:04:27,330
- that depends on the ritual.

69
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:30,850
When the ritual is successfully performed,
several things happen:

70
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:33,910
First, the members feeling of belonging to
this group is strengthened.

71
00:04:33,910 --> 00:04:38,250
In particular, the feeling for what helps
or hurts the group is reinforced.

72
00:04:38,250 --> 00:04:43,350
In other words, the group establishes its
sense of good and bad, its morality.

73
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:47,800
Secondly the participants gain "emotional
energy" - in other words, the ritual establishes

74
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:49,770
a virtual happyfield of its own.

75
00:04:49,770 --> 00:04:53,180
But you may ask, what happens when the ritual
is over?

76
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:57,230
The happyfield will collapse, the group spirit
will vanish, and then?

77
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:00,640
Wouldn't it be great to have some keepsake
of the rituals, something the members can

78
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,060
take home?

79
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:04,600
That's where the third effect comes in: Symbols.

80
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:09,160
A symbol is some kind of portable happyfield
capacitor to conserve the ritual happyfield.

81
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:14,330
It can be an actual object, a sign, an idea,
a person, a word or anything else.

82
00:05:14,330 --> 00:05:18,470
Often, the symbol is actually the sacred object
the ritual was about.

83
00:05:18,470 --> 00:05:22,190
It's a bit like a souvenir - those little
trinkets earthlings bring back home from a

84
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:24,530
voyage as a keepsake of good moments.

85
00:05:24,530 --> 00:05:29,040
A symbol doesn't only make happy, but also
serves as a reminder for shared emotions,

86
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:32,030
group spirit and group morality - till the
next ritual.

87
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:34,720
Because there is always a next ritual.

88
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:38,949
Rituals are repeated more or less frequently
- this is called ritual chains.

89
00:05:38,949 --> 00:05:43,300
Each group has its own ritual chains where
the goup reinfoces its coherency, establishes

90
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,680
its moral values and creates and charges its
symbols.

91
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:49,890
All rituals consist of the same components
we just identified:

92
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:52,139
- a group of earthlings gathering together,

93
00:05:52,139 --> 00:05:53,930
- Predefined formal rules,

94
00:05:53,930 --> 00:05:55,569
- concentration on a sacret object,

95
00:05:55,569 --> 00:05:56,590
- shared emotions,

96
00:05:56,590 --> 00:05:59,250
- and coordinated, often rhythmic actions.

97
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:00,869
On the outcome side, we have

98
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:03,330
- positive emotions, like happyness or solace

99
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:05,460
- a reinforced group spirit,

100
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:07,400
- establishment of moral group standards,

101
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:10,870
- and the charging of group spirit capacitors
- symbols.

102
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:14,970
Modern earthlings often display "their" symbols
on t-shirts, casual clothing they wear on

103
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:15,980
the upper body.

104
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:21,220
"Casual" means that the clothing is not required
by formal dress-codes of other ritual chains.

105
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:26,050
As a rule of thumb, when you can put something
onto a t-shirt, it's probably a symbol.

106
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,880
By the way, in case my earthlings viewers
want t-shirts with symbols from my show, I've

107
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:32,990
created an online t-shirt shop.

108
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:35,590
You'll find the link in the doobly-doo.

109
00:06:35,590 --> 00:06:37,340
Symbols don't need to be graphical.

110
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,870
Intimate sexual relationships, for example,
are built and maintained by ritual chains

111
00:06:41,870 --> 00:06:47,740
called "romance" which create all kinds of
symbols: Words, objects, plants, even sounds

112
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:50,240
- often called, "our song".

113
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:54,889
Acoustical symbols are also used by large
groups, like nations.

114
00:06:54,889 --> 00:06:57,610
National musical symbols are called "anthems".

115
00:06:57,610 --> 00:07:03,660
Other national symbols include famous earthlings,
food, drinks, clothes, historical events,

116
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:06,330
words, colors, shapes and creatures.

117
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:09,580
Actually, almost everything can become a national
symbol.

118
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:12,910
[*microbes]
Except for the microbes.

119
00:07:12,910 --> 00:07:16,350
Tips for tourists.

120
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:20,139
A concert is a strange but entertaining type
of ritual.

121
00:07:20,139 --> 00:07:24,759
If you do what the other spectators do, you
may stay unnoticed in the crowd.

122
00:07:24,759 --> 00:07:28,630
However, don't imitate the musicians, you
might get into trouble.

123
00:07:28,630 --> 00:07:31,630
Let's look at a common ritual: A public ball
game.

124
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:35,570
Usually, games are performed in series - ritual
chains.

125
00:07:35,570 --> 00:07:38,130
Here we have player teams and fans gathered
together.

126
00:07:38,130 --> 00:07:41,620
The players perform a highly ritualized game
which follows strict rules and requires a

127
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,250
lot of coordination.

128
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:48,070
But the fans do also coordinated, rhythmic
actions, like chanting or choreographic wave

129
00:07:48,070 --> 00:07:51,160
movements, to express their shared emotions.

130
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:55,900
For the players, the sacret object is the
ball, for the fans it's rather "their" team.

131
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:59,850
On the outcome side, we have an emotional
happyfield, a reinforced group spirit and

132
00:07:59,850 --> 00:08:04,810
often shared feelings of morality, for example
against referees perceived as unfair.

133
00:08:04,810 --> 00:08:09,400
And to maintain these achievements after the
ritual, there are the symbols: Team emblems,

134
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:14,220
color codes, and sometimes a trophy for the
winning team.

135
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:15,220
Scientific advice.

136
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:19,310
The advantage of open air rituals is that
they are easy to observe from high altitude.

137
00:08:19,310 --> 00:08:23,770
If you want to learn what happens when the
ritual is disturbed, just beam a cow onto

138
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:28,280
the playing field and observe the reaction.

139
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:30,979
Perhaps the most common rituals are conversations.

140
00:08:30,979 --> 00:08:35,240
A conversation often starts with a greeting
- a ritual we already encountered in episode

141
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:36,279
7.

142
00:08:36,279 --> 00:08:41,029
Greetings usually involve touching each other
in a synchronized, even choreographic manner.

143
00:08:41,029 --> 00:08:45,390
The actual conversation may start with the
question "How are you", in order to synchronize

144
00:08:45,390 --> 00:08:46,390
emotions.

145
00:08:46,390 --> 00:08:50,500
What follows is a turn-taking exchange of
sentences that may or may not carry actual

146
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:55,320
information - sometimes earthlings just speak
about atmospheric conditions or events everybody

147
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:57,320
has already heard of.

148
00:08:57,320 --> 00:08:59,130
Earthlings call this "small talk".

149
00:08:59,130 --> 00:09:03,380
The goal of small talk is not information
sharing, but sharing of emotions and the activity

150
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,590
of turn-talking speaking itself.

151
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:09,850
In Great Britain and America, gaps or overlaps
between turns in a friendly conversation are

152
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:12,620
typically shorter than a tenth of a second.

153
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:16,740
Longer gaps are perceived as "awkward", longer
overlaps as "unfriendly".

154
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:21,880
Sometimes, conversation rituals lead to judging
outsiders as immoral or awkward.

155
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:24,220
This kind of conversation is called "gossip".

156
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:28,220
This is an example for rituals strengthening
a sense of morality.

157
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:32,330
In larger groups, this can grow to a public
outrage against other groups, which can lead

158
00:09:32,330 --> 00:09:34,080
to riots and pogroms.

159
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:38,690
A recent example for those hate rituals is
that of public protests in France by a conservative

160
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:43,610
minority, against a law that gives homosexuals
the right to do something called marriage,

161
00:09:43,610 --> 00:09:46,010
formerly a privilege of heterosexuals.

162
00:09:46,010 --> 00:09:50,020
As a general rule, Earthlings don't like to
share their privileges.

163
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:53,040
Marriage is a formal act to officialise a
sexual relationship.

164
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:55,380
But why is this formality so important?

165
00:09:55,380 --> 00:10:00,770
The answer is simple: It's a ritual!

166
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:04,820
The marriage ritual strengthens not only the
bond of the couple itself, but also admids

167
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:07,520
the couple to the group of married citizens.

168
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:11,500
And many members of this group don't want
homosexuals in their club.

169
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:15,810
An important kind of rituals is punishments,
unpleasant actions imposed upon individuals

170
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:18,620
that have violated the moral standards of
the group.

171
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:24,260
That can be a monetary penalty, imprisonment,
torture, public humiliation, exile or death.

172
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:28,640
The goal is not only to discourage violators,
but also to ritually restitute the damaged

173
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:30,380
group integrity.

174
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:32,410
Earthlings call this "justice".

175
00:10:32,410 --> 00:10:36,250
Another important type is rituals of passage,
which mark earthlings' transition to a new

176
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:43,630
period of life: Being born, entering adulthood,
graduating from school, marriage, death.

177
00:10:43,630 --> 00:10:48,480
Entering a social group can also be made by
a rituals of passage - those are called initiation

178
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:49,480
rituals.

179
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:53,870
Rites of passage often include physical pain,
fear or another kind of suffering - from simple

180
00:10:53,870 --> 00:10:57,660
practical jokes up to life-threatening ordeals.

181
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:02,060
That's also the reason why final exams at
schools or universities are usually very stressful

182
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:06,930
- even if the candidate has already proven
their ability by writing a scientific paper.

183
00:11:06,930 --> 00:11:11,610
Exams are rites of passage, they have to hurt!

184
00:11:11,610 --> 00:11:13,430
Strategic Advice.

185
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:17,730
Conquering earth isn't really difficult, given
your technological superiority.

186
00:11:17,730 --> 00:11:21,100
But ruling the planet afterwards is a wholly
differnt thing.

187
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:24,700
To stay in power, you will need mass rituals
and strong symbols.

188
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:30,709
However, chose your symbols carefully: Offensive
or funny symbols may ruin your authority.

189
00:11:30,709 --> 00:11:35,680
This episode is mainly based upon the book
"Interaction ritual chains" by Randall Collins.

190
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:38,740
According to the book, rituals require bodily
presence.

191
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:41,209
However, I'm not sure about that.

192
00:11:41,209 --> 00:11:45,270
In the recent years, one could observe the
rise of strong online communities which create

193
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:49,610
their own group identity, group symbols and
a common sense of morality, even if they never

194
00:11:49,610 --> 00:11:51,029
meet in person.

195
00:11:51,029 --> 00:11:55,110
This might indicate the existance of some
online equivalent to interaction rituals,

196
00:11:55,110 --> 00:11:57,820
which hold those communities together.

197
00:11:57,820 --> 00:12:01,600
So, Earthlings use rituals for pretty much
every social activity.

198
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:02,720
But why?

199
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:04,360
What's the purpose of rituals?

200
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:07,660
I think rituals are actually a substitute
for telepathy.

201
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:12,920
See, like most social civilizations, earthlings
have different perceived planes of reality:

202
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:17,770
The physical world, the plane of emotions,
and social reality, which includes things

203
00:12:17,770 --> 00:12:23,731
like social status, relationships, groups,
gratitude, karma etc. - all those mechanisms

204
00:12:23,731 --> 00:12:25,270
which make up society.

205
00:12:25,270 --> 00:12:28,110
Actually, this is the plane where society
happens.

206
00:12:28,110 --> 00:12:33,060
There is a fourth plane, the legal reality
- a formalization of some aspects of social

207
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:34,060
reality.

208
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:36,330
I'll speak about this in another episode.

209
00:12:36,330 --> 00:12:40,209
Normally, a civilization would manage those
pkanes via telepathy.

210
00:12:40,209 --> 00:12:45,421
The Yetlings of Arcturus 11, for example,
are driven by some kind of emotions, form

211
00:12:45,421 --> 00:12:50,950
groups and personal relationships, and have
a virtual exchange currency, not unlike gratitude,

212
00:12:50,950 --> 00:12:52,390
but shared within a group.

213
00:12:52,390 --> 00:12:57,100
Now, being emotion-driven beings, Yetlings
need to share emotions telepathically.

214
00:12:57,100 --> 00:13:01,380
Also, they use telepathy to act on the social
plane.

215
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:03,450
Mechanisms like this are common in the galaxy.

216
00:13:03,450 --> 00:13:07,770
Some alien scientists have even theorized
that a society isn't possible without telepathy,

217
00:13:07,770 --> 00:13:11,430
because only telepathy allows the establishment
and continuous synchronization of a common

218
00:13:11,430 --> 00:13:13,410
dynamic social reality.

219
00:13:13,410 --> 00:13:18,660
The common telepathic consciousness provides,
so they say, the medium where the social reality

220
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:19,660
happens.

221
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:24,890
This sounds quite convincing, in theory - but
then there is this big, annoying, paradoxical

222
00:13:24,890 --> 00:13:26,630
example: Earthlings.

223
00:13:26,630 --> 00:13:31,260
Highly social beings, which built up a complex
society without any telepathy - how is this

224
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:32,610
possible?

225
00:13:32,610 --> 00:13:35,290
The answer is simple: Rituals.

226
00:13:35,290 --> 00:13:38,670
Symbols and rituals provide the connection
between reality and other perceived planes

227
00:13:38,670 --> 00:13:40,050
of existence.

228
00:13:40,050 --> 00:13:43,800
When earthlings want to synchronize emotions,
they use rituals.

229
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:48,810
When they want to act upon groups, status,
gratitude, relationships etc., they do it

230
00:13:48,810 --> 00:13:50,320
through rituals.

231
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:53,269
Even actions on the legal level require rituals.

232
00:13:53,269 --> 00:13:56,920
Earthling society as we know it wouldn't be
possible without rituals.

233
00:13:56,920 --> 00:14:01,240
Of course, earthlings don't do rituals because
they are important for society - they do them

234
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:02,940
because they enjoy them.

235
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:06,500
And whenever earthlings enjoy something, you
can be pretty sure that it's again a scheme

236
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:09,660
of our old friend, the genetic imperative.

237
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:11,270
What a surprise.

238
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:14,190
A note concerning my closed AdSense account.

239
00:14:14,190 --> 00:14:17,490
First of all, thank you all for your encouraging
messages and ideas.

240
00:14:17,490 --> 00:14:22,910
However, I decided not to look for alternative
ways to monetize my videos, except the t-shirt

241
00:14:22,910 --> 00:14:23,910
shop.

242
00:14:23,910 --> 00:14:28,190
Anyway, I don't do this for a living - this
is what earthlings call a hobby.

243
00:14:28,190 --> 00:14:30,970
This was the eleventh episode of Earthlings
101.

244
00:14:30,970 --> 00:14:35,420
In the next episode we will learn about the
earthlings' most important sense: Vision.

245
00:14:35,420 --> 00:14:38,970
We will also learn why earthlings call this
"yellow", although it's actually a pattern

246
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:41,160
of red and green.

247
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:45,460
Thanks for watching, click on "like" if you
enjoyed the video, and as always: Don't forget

248
00:14:45,460 --> 00:15:04,680
to be alien!

